  "kind": "movement",
  "title": "Spread of Islam in Southeast Asia",
  "summary": "Islamic communities appeared with a political profile in the thirteenth century as port city-states ruled by sultans, the earliest being Pasai in North Sumatra, whose ruler Malik as-Salih probably converted around 1282. Muslim centres spread the faith through preaching, trade, alliances and intermarriage. Melaka controlled the passage between the Indian Ocean and the China Seas by the mid-15th century, and by 1500 Islamic enclaves stood from Sumatra to Manila. Southeast Asia now holds at least one-fifth of the world's Muslims.",
